  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 AM MDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nShort wave ridge has moved east of our area as incoming Pacific \ntrough (just moving onshore of the west coast) approaches the \nwestern US. As the southwest flow increases ahead of this trough, \ntropical moisture from both TS Odalys and Hurricane Polo will \nstream across the area. At this moment, Odalys is contributing \nmost of the moisture, with high clouds over our area and lower \nclouds/precip well further west. Moisture ahead of Polo will \nslowly advect into southern portions of the CWA late this \nafternoon and evening. Some of the hi-res models (and GFS/NAM12) \nare showing some precip over SW New Mexico late this \nafternoon/evening, so did put low POPs in grids for this area. \n\nMonday through Wednesday...the main event starts with heavy rain \nand flash flood potential. The Pacific trough moves over SoCal \nwith a 90+ kt sub-tropical jet rotating around its base over \nArizona and far western New Mexico. This will increase the layered\ntropical moisture over the CWA, with PWs approaching 1.5 inches \nover southern New Mexico Monday afternoon. Showers and isolated \nthunderstorms should develop Monday afternoon, with a few areas of\nheavy rainfall. The remnants of Polo reach the CWA Tuesday \nafternoon (latest NAM12/ECMWF are a bit slower than GFS), with PWs\nstill maxing out around 1.5 inches. This is about 240% of normal,\nwhile daily PW records at KEPZ are around 1.3 inches. Whatever is\nleft of the Polo remnants lifts over the Texas Panhandle Tuesday \nnight as the Pacific trough nears the Arizona/New Mexico state \nline. Moisture will be on the wain, with PWs rapidly falling below\none inch. By Wednesday afternoon the Pacific trough is over NE \nNew Mexico and approaching the central Plains by late Wednesday \nnight. Rain should taper off to scattered showers Wednesday \nafternoon as wrap around moisture from the Pacific trough \npersists.\n\nThursday into the weekend...high aloft builds over the Desert \nSouthwest for mainly dry weather with temperatures on the cool \nside. Both GFS/ECMWF show the Pacific trough becoming positively \ntilted and try to retrograde a circulation back to the southwest. \nThis could drop a few showers over Otero and Hudspeth Counties. \nGFS moves what will be Rachel over the Baja by the end of the \nweekend and possibly spreading some rain to the area--ECMWF keeps \nRachel well to the west. \n\n***Bottom line for weather/impacts:*** Flood Watch remains in \n effect from Monday afternoon to early Wednesday morning. Expect \n areas of heavy rain to develop late Monday afternoon and continue\n into late Tuesday night/early Wednesday morning, along with a \n moderate risk of flash flooding. Confidence is still lower on \n areal distribution of the rainfall. What we can see from the \n models is the rainfall oriented in a SW- NE pattern associated \n with mid/upper level flow. Many of the models are showing the \n heavier rainfall from about a line from Columbus- Alamogordo \n northwest, with the heaviest rainfall over Sierra, Luna, Hidalgo \n and eastern Grant Counties; generally 1-3 inches with a few areas\n possibly receiving up to 4 inches. To the areas southeast of \n this line, rainfall of .5 to 2 inches, with local areas (mainly \n the Sacramento Mtns) seeing 3-4 inches.",
